.TH RATRACE 1
.SH NAME
ratrace \- trace process system calls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ratrace
[
.I pid
] | [
.I -c command
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ratrace
shows the system calls executed by a process,
either the one with
.I pid
or a fresh invocation of
.IR command .
.PP
Trace output is determined by the kernel, not
.IR ratrace .
Certain fixed rules apply.
The first four fields of the output are
pid, text name, system call name, and the PC of the user program.
Data is always printed as
.IB pointer /\c
"\fIstring\fP",
where the
.I string
is the first 64 bytes of the data, with
.L \&.
replacing non-printing ASCII characters
(printing characters are those between ASCII space (SP) and delete (DEL), exclusive).
Return values follow an
.LR = ,
and include the integer return value,
the
.I errstr
(with "" if there is no
.IR errstr ),
and
the start and stop times for the system call in nanoseconds.
The times are exclusive of the overhead for tracing.
A call's start and its end are two writes, so the output of processes traced at once can interleave
within a line.
.SS Boot trace
.I Ratrace
attaches to a process that already runs,
so the kernel traces the boot itself:
every process from the first, pid 1, in the same format,
a line for each call, written when the call ends
(an
.B Exits
line when it starts).
Under Node,
.IP
.EX
node k/build/src/main/node.js --ratrace=\fIfile\fP -- \fIcommand\fP
.EE
.PP
writes the trace to the host's
.IR file .
On the page,
.B ?ratrace
in its URL, or a line
.B ratrace=1
in its
.IR plan9.ini (8),
turns it on.
Either way the kernel keeps the trace, up to 64 megabytes, in
.B /dev/ratrace
(see
.IR cons (3)).
.SH FILES
.BI /proc/ pid /syscall
.br
.BI /proc/ pid /ctl
.br
.B /dev/ratrace
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/ratrace.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR acid (1),
.IR db (1),
.IR proc (3)
.SH BUGS
The printing of the data is too limited in length;
printing
.L \&.
instead of something more sensible is limiting.
.PP
Tracing ends when the first traced process exits:
its reader calls
.IR threadexitsall .
A child's first call shows only its end.
In p9web the program counter is always 0.
